# sphere_rough.cam — first stock_to_leave roughing fixture (DECISIONS.md
# "stock_to_leave first slice…"). The sphere_scallop.cam sphere run as a ROUGHING
# pass: the 0.5mm allowance is a ceiling on TOTAL remaining material including
# this pass's own cusps (max, not min), so scallop=0.2 spends 0.2mm of it as
# cusp budget and only the remaining 0.3mm becomes nominal skin — the pass
# machines the inflated sphere r=20.3, every CL point 23.3mm from the centre
# (apex plunge Z=23.3, the hand-checked anchor). Constant scallop on purpose:
# waterline stepover near a dome apex leaves cusps that outspend a realistic
# allowance outright (the error tests pin that), so scallop is the spacing a
# curved-top roughing pass actually gets to use.
#
# CONTAINED 2026-08-13 (DECISIONS.md "rim-area accounting…", slice 2):
# the stock used to stop AT this feature's base plane, so the whole
# surface stood in air and the fixture only ran because there was
# nothing to hit. The stock now stands 2mm proud of the feature's top
# (z 22) and T1 roughs the moat before the finish;
# numbers in the older comments describe the same toolpath, whose
# motion is unchanged where it touches the surface.
units mm
feed 600
plunge 200
rapid_z 25
clearance_z 40
spindle 4000

stock Box(w=100, l=80, h=47, x=0, y=0, z=22)

tool T1 Endmill(dia=12.0)
tool T9 Ballmill(dia=6.0)

# Sphere centre (equator plane) at z=0; the machinable dome rises to z=20 (r=20).
feature TopDome Sphere(dia=40, x=50, y=40, z=0)

run Clear(tool=T1, profile=TopDome, stepover=8.0, stepdown=5.0, stock_to_leave=1)
run Copy(tool=T9, surface=TopDome, scallop=0.2, stock_to_leave=0.5)
